1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,944
Chapter sixteen — the cost of meetings.

2
00:00:04,004 --> 00:00:10,947
Every organization has a ledger nobody
keeps: the hours its ceremonies consume.

3
00:00:11,007 --> 00:00:16,035
The workbench keeps it.

4
00:00:16,095 --> 00:00:29,261
A meeting is a node. It has a cadence,
a duration, and an attendee list.

5
00:00:29,321 --> 00:00:38,766
Attendance is an edge, so the cost
lands on the people who actually attend.

6
00:00:38,826 --> 00:00:44,871
Roles supply hours. Ceremonies consume them.

7
00:00:44,931 --> 00:00:55,779
Capacity minus ceremonies is effective
capacity — what is left to do the work.

8
00:00:55,839 --> 00:01:05,784
Fifty-six people on the books. Fifty-three
point nine effective, after ceremonies.

9
00:01:05,844 --> 00:01:16,789
Three hundred and sixty-one hours a month, in
nine ceremonies. Forty thousand dollars of it.

10
00:01:16,849 --> 00:01:31,800
That is the coordination tax,
and it is computed, not guessed.

11
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:43,631
The report ranks the roles by how
much of their week the calendar takes.

12
00:01:43,691 --> 00:01:50,191
Cut a ceremony and the hours come
back — with a number attached.
